ecked the pods and saw that these MongoDB pods are in Pending status. The other services are running fine.\n\nSo, why are these MongoDB pods pending? The next step is to check the events related to these pods. Pending usually means that the pod hasn\'t been scheduled yet. Possible reasons could be resource constraints, node selectors not matching, or image pull issues. \n\nThe tool \'kubectl describe pod\' can give more details about why a pod is pending. By describing the specific MongoDB pods, we can look into the events and see if there\'s an error message indicating the root cause. For example, if there\'s a message about insufficient CPU or memory, that\'s a resource issue. Or if the pod can\'t pull the image, that\'s another problem. \n\nSo, the best tool here is \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' with the command \'kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation\'. Let\'s pick one of the pending pods, like mongodb-geo, and describe it to get more information.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The MongoDB pods (e.g., mongodb-geo) are in a "Pending" state, which typically indicates scheduling issues. To diagnose the root cause, I need to inspect the pod\'s detailed events and status. Using `kubectl describe pod` will reveal reasons like resource constraints, image pull failures, node selectors, or taints preventing scheduling. This will help identify the specific issue preventing the pods from running.  \n\n<tool_call>  \n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod mongodb-geo-574794b9fb-9dzm6 -n hotel-reservation"}}  \n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 406, 'prompt_tokens': 4368, 'total_tokens': 4774, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc7-9052-7f10-8e66-7a7891bb03f0-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4368, 'output_tokens': 406, 'total_tokens': 4774}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='79d39d48-9024-4770-bc97-c4604e33d3cb'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to figure out why the MongoDB pods are in a Pending state. They already checked the deployments and saw that the replicas are 0/1, which means the pods aren't running. Then they tried to get the pod logs but got a Not Found error, so they checked the list of pods again and saw that the MongoDB pods are indeed Pending.\n\nNow, the next step is to use kubectl describe pod to get more details about why the pod is pending. The user chose that command, and the response from the tool will show events and status reasons. Common issues for Pending pods include resource limits, image pull errors, node selectors not matching, or taints on the nodes. By describing the pod, they can check the Events section for specific error messages. For example, if there's an ImagePullBackOff, it means the container image can't be pulled. Or if there's a scheduling problem, maybe the nodes don't have enough resources or have taints that prevent the pod from being scheduled. The describe command will provide all that info, which is crucial for pinpointing the root cause. Once they have that info, they can determine the exact problem and suggest the necessary fix, like adjusting resource requests, fixing image names, or modifying node taints.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod mongodb-geo-574794b9fb-9dzm6 -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ad5c7cb82e3991c1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, respo